> We should be able to do the interdiff deterministically rather than via
> inference. The previous version is known, so it's possible to fetch the
> SHA, or apply the patches if necessary, to get the old version and
> perform the interdiff with the new version.

Posted as "[PATCH v2 0/4] Require an interdiff on revised series" [2],
on b/dev@upstream. It applies the patches rather than fetching the
SHA. The commits behind a posted version are often rewritten or never
existed in our trees, while the mail always exists and is what you
reviewed.

- series-rebuild.sh takes the Message-ID of a cover letter or a
  single patch. It applies the posted patches onto their base-commit,
  or onto another commit with -o, and prints the range. Each commit
  is dated from its message, so the same mail always rebuilds to the
  same ids.
- format-patch.sh refuses -v N for N > 1 without --previous=<id>. It
  rebuilds the previous version onto the new --base and interdiffs
  against that, so a rebase does not fill the interdiff with
  everything that landed between the two bases.
- prompt.md, the mail workflow guide, and the freebsd-port skill say
  how to use it.

I checked that it is deterministic. Rebuilding each signoz version
from the list gave a tree identical to the commit it was formatted
from. v2 was formatted with the new script, and rebuilds to its own
tree too.

v1 of that series no longer applies to b/dev, because its second
patch edited the process-email skill, which b/dev removed. So
format-patch.sh fell back to a range-diff, as designed, and the
pairing in it is poor. If you would rather have the interdiff across
both bases even when it is noisy, I can make the fallback emit both.

> Additionally, respond to the cover letters for the signoz patch series
> with interdiffs, essentially backfilling what should have been there in
> the first place.

Done. Each signoz revision was a single patch with no cover letter, so
I replied to the patches of v2 through v6 with an interdiff against
